          ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2t-1,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t-16\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+2t-16 & =0 \\\\ (3t+8)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{8}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(-\\frac{8}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{8}{3})-1=-\\frac{19}{3}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(2) & =2(2)-1=3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and $t=2.$",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/id": "8d5a1c2d-a7e0-54a8-a5a7-341e3117f428"
        },
