          ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3,\\qquad y'(t)=-9{t}^{2}-72t-144.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{t}^{2}-72t-144 & =0 \\\\ -9(t+4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-4) & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$.",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/id": "214e5498-f36d-52fa-9f5b-0df198175ccc"
        },
