          ],
          ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =5\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/id": "c4aa422d-47db-5c0f-a9d9-d1448ec6867d"
        },
